---
description: Core data-flow architecture for the elixir project — enforced on every file in lib/data/ and lib/src/.
alwaysApply: true
---

## Canonical data flow

```
UI  (lib/src/<feature>/)
 └─> Provider  (lib/data/providers/<domain>_provider.dart)
      ├─> Client  (lib/data/services/clients/<domain>_client.dart)
      └─> [when needed]  DataSources  (lib/data/db/, lib/data/shared_preference/, lib/data/hubs/)
```

**Providers call Retrofit clients directly.** There is no repository layer in this project — provider -> client is the only correct path. Multi-step orchestration, mapping, caching, or local persistence all live inside the provider.

If a domain needs non-trivial coordination (e.g. uploading attachments before posting, refreshing dependent providers, talking to a datasource), that logic lives inside the same `<domain>_provider.dart` file alongside the rest of that domain's providers.

## Swagger controller → file naming

For each Swagger controller use one matching `snake_case` base name (e.g. `Auth` → `auth`):

| Layer | Path | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Retrofit client | `lib/data/services/clients/<name>_client.dart` | `auth_client.dart` |
| Riverpod provider | `lib/data/providers/<name>_provider.dart` | `auth_provider.dart` |

Do not add a separate provider file per UI page or UI step. Put related notifiers in the same domain `*_provider.dart` file — multiple `@riverpod` classes in one file is fine.

## Layer rules

### Provider (`lib/data/providers/`)
- Use `@riverpod` codegen + `part '*.g.dart'` — do not hand-write providers.
- Call the Retrofit client for the domain.
- May read other providers when orchestration is required.
- Never call datasources directly when a client exists for the data; if a feature genuinely needs a datasource (e.g. `objectBoxStoreProvider`, `sharedPreferencesProvider`), the provider may consume it directly.
- Never import from `lib/src/` (no UI types, no `BuildContext` in pure data flow — the only exception is helpers that explicitly need to drive a `FilePlugin`/picker call from a button handler, which is documented in the affected provider).
- Expose immutable state only; all mutations happen inside notifier methods.

### Retrofit client (`lib/data/services/clients/`)
- Stateless: typed HTTP endpoints only, no business logic.
- All Retrofit clients live under `lib/data/services/clients/`. Do not create a parallel `lib/data/clients/` folder.
- Shared Retrofit helpers (Dio module, interceptors, converters) stay in `lib/data/services/http/` and `lib/data/services/interceptors/`.

### Datasources (`lib/data/db/`, `lib/data/shared_preference/`, `lib/data/hubs/`)
- Low-level persistence/transport only.
- No transformations, no UI awareness.

## Mapping rule (data/models in UI)

This codebase ships with a large amount of UI that imports `lib/data/models/` directly. We are deliberately **not** doing a big-bang migration; the policy is pragmatic, not absolute:

- **New features** must never consume raw API models from `lib/data/models/` in `lib/src/`. Map inside the provider into a feature-facing type when the UI needs a different shape.
- **Feature-facing types** may live in `lib/src/<feature>/models/` only when a dedicated UI shape is needed and it does not simply duplicate the API model.
- **Existing UI** that already consumes `lib/data/models/` directly is grandfathered in. Do not refactor in isolation — that creates noisy diffs and parallel models. Migrate opportunistically when you are already touching the file for another reason (bug fix, redesign, behavioural change).
- **Never** introduce a new alias/wrapper model that just re-exports a `lib/data/models/` type. If the UI shape matches the API, keep using the API model rather than duplicating it.
- When in doubt, prefer extending the existing data model with computed getters over creating a parallel feature model.

## Forbidden patterns
- UI calling a Retrofit client or datasource directly.
- Reintroducing a `lib/data/repositories/` folder — that layer has been deleted on purpose.
- API models imported in `lib/src/` widget files for newly added features.
- New abstraction layers: no `usecases/`, `controllers/`, `managers/`, `viewmodels/`, or `repositories/` folders anywhere.
- Placing providers under `lib/src/<feature>/` — providers belong in `lib/data/providers/` only.
